WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.870
In the inaugural season of Netflix's The Witcher,
the Law of Surprise is the hinge upon which

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:09.330
the entire plot twists but for those coming
to the series having no familiarity with the

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.180
property, it may seem a bit confusing.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.320
What does the Law of Surprise mean to Geralt
and his world?

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:18.700
The concept has taken many forms across a
lot of cultures' folklore, but it basically

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:24.140
boils down to this: in exchange for an unpayable
debt usually, saving someone's life the person

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.580
who invokes the law is demanding the benefit
of the target's unexpected luck.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.060
This can be anything, but the trick is that
the item is bestowed by luck and fate, and

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.880
is unknown to the person giving the item away
until they return home safely.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.300
"Find Geralt of Rivia.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.780
He is your destiny."

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.340
Many parables involve the trope of a nobleman
traveling and having their life saved by someone

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:49.060
of lower birth.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.580
Rather than accept the immediacy of gold or
other form of material wealth, the peasant

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.490
will invoke the Law of Surprise, which leads
to the nobleman having to give up something

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:58.740
far more precious.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.600
When the nobleman grows selfish and attempts
to thwart this invocation of fate by either

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.400
trying to pay the peasant off or attempting
to kill them, terrible things befall them.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.200
Tales using this trope are meant to highlight
the virtues of selflessness and humility good

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.340
people and kings honor their bargains, even
when the cost is exceedingly high, and the

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.280
universe should punish those who do not.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:18.920
"The Law of Surprise has been called.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.040
You kill them, you kill me."

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.509
Here's how the Law of Surprise comes into
play in the first season of The Witcher.

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:33.439
Many years before the events of the series,
Lord Urcheon, humble knight of the realm,

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.420
saves the king from death.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.380
With extreme humility, Urcheon insists he
needs no material benefit for doing so, but

00:01:39.389 --> 00:01:43.959
upon the king's continued pressure, chooses
the Law of Surprise as his repayment, never

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.030
once imagining it would mean Calanthe's unborn
child.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.860
Having been cursed to look like a hedgehog
from a young age, he did his best to stave

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:54.920
off the fate the Law of Surprise demanded
until his curse lifted hearing the twelfth

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.800
peal of a bell in the course of his life.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.780
Fate, of course, intervenes, and Urcheon meets
Princess Pavetta seemingly by chance, and

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:02.280
they fall in love.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:06.600
Their secret betrothal is what drives him
to interrupt the Feast of Cintra, demanding

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.180
what is his by ancient right.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:09.780
"Lioness of Cintra!

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.500
I come to claim what is rightfully mine!

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:14.800
Pavetta.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.020
By the Law of Surprise."

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.500
Queen Calanthe gets mad, there's that big
fight, and Geralt steps in directly to save

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.200
Urcheon's life twice in succession.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.280
When Calanthe finally relents, Urcheon insists
just as the king had done years before to

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.960
repay Geralt for his intervention.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.420
Geralt, believing all this to be traditionalist
nonsense, chooses the Law of Surprise over

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:39.000
literally everyone else's interruption that
maybe, Geralt buddy, you should think it through.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.720
Alas, he does not, and the cheeky revelation
of Pavetta vomiting immediately afterward

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:44.900
reveals her pregnancy.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:49.159
Ciri is on the way, and Geralt refuses to
recognize what he has wrought for himself.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.800
He mocks the concept of destiny openly over
his friend Mousesack's warnings, and leaves...

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.300
to his continued and countless future troubles.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.260
It's only when he accepts his responsibility
under the Law of Surprise that he is put at

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:00.760
ease.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:05.300
Geralt is sympathetic; he is a man of forced
circumstances, living a life he had no choice

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.680
in and basically waiting to die, though he
strives to at least be effective in his profession.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.960
His scintilla of hope for something better
through Yennefer is lost precisely because

00:03:13.969 --> 00:03:17.879
of the selfishness through which he employed
the Law of Surprise thinking he'd cheated

00:03:17.879 --> 00:03:21.600
someone out of a child he could never bear
himself with no consequences, and when it

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.160
got tough, abandoning Ciri to a
much uglier fate than necessary.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:29.500
Both Geralt and Calanthe thought destiny was
hogwash, tried to abuse others' belief in

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.340
it for their own ends, and ended up severely
punished for it.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.390
"But you can't outrun destiny just because
you're terrified of it.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:37.980
It's coming, Geralt.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:40.960
Not believing won't change that."

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.560
The punishment is what engenders belief in
fate but too late for Calanthe, and almost

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:46.200
so for Geralt.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.880
Ciri is consequential both because of the
potent magical bloodline she belongs to, as

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.980
well as her unknown attachment to Geralt through
what amounts to his own selfish stupidity.

00:03:55.629 --> 00:03:59.100
It's much more than a simple bit of moral
instruction to the world of The Witcher: it's

00:03:59.111 --> 00:04:03.269
magic making itself known in its world, and
the mortals within it are merely shifting

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:04.560
among its tides.

